          "start": 16.95999999999999,
          "end": 16.97999999999999,
          "score": 0
        }
      ],
      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's the key. Each dye particle keeps switching between two places. It can stick to the paper and stay put, or it can dissolve in the moving water and get carried up. A dye that's attracted more strongly to the paper spends more of its time stuck, so it falls behind and travels a shorter distance. A dye that dissolves well in the water, and sticks only weakly to the paper, spends more of its time moving, so it travels further. Every dye had the same time, so different attractions spread them apart. That's why one black dot becomes three spots.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
