          "word": "solvent's",
          "start": 19.965000000000078,
          "end": 20.34700000000008,
          "score": 0.482
        }
      ],
      "requestAttempts": 2,
      "pronunciationText": "Why a ratio? If you'd stopped this run halfway, the front would be at four point zero centimetres, P at one point two and Q at three point two. Every distance halves, but the ratios stay at zero point three zero and zero point eight zero. Each spot moves a fixed fraction of the solvent's distance. But that fraction depends on the conditions, the type of paper, the solvent and the temperature. Change any of them, and the R F values change.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
