          "score": 0.367
        }
      ],
      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "That's how chromatography helps identify substances. Run known substances, called standards, on the same paper, in the same solvent, at the same time. Here, standard X has an R F of zero point three zero, and standard Y has zero point eight zero. So spot P matches X, and spot Q matches Y. But a match supports the identity, it doesn't prove it, because two different substances can share an R F value. To be more confident, run them again in a different solvent, and check they still match.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
