      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "It's the blue one, lowest on the strip, because it spent the most time stuck. To compare spots fairly, chemists use a ratio called the R F value. It's the distance the spot moved, divided by the distance the solvent moved. Both are measured from the baseline, the pencil line where the sample started. For the spot, measure to its centre. For the solvent, measure to the solvent front, the highest point the solvent reached. A spot can never get ahead of the solvent, so R F is always between zero and one, and it has no units.",
